  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nWednesday - Monday:\nAs we progress through mid-week, ensemble guidance highlights a\nslight dip in the jet stream. The trends over the last few days\nremain intact, with PoPs being suppressed north of the area. The\nhighest chances (10-15%) nudge into portions of central MO on\nWednesday and Thursday, though confidence is low. A closer look\nat the latest RRFS, REFS, and HREF suggest an isolated shower or\nthunderstorm clipping the area, though the majority of the \nguidance keeps it further north.\n\nOtherwise, the storyline remains extreme heat persisting through \nlate week and this weekend. NBM interquartile spreads on max\nhigh temperatures remain around 2 to 3 degrees in the upper 90s\nto near 100. Accompanied by dewpoints around 70, maximum daily \nheat index values are forecast to be around around 100 to 105+.\nThis introduces the question, how extreme is this heat from a \nclimatological stance. While the area is no stranger to heat \nwaves in the Summer, the duration of the heat over several days \ncan compound the impacts. NWS HeatRisk captures this potential \nwith widespread Major to Extreme heat risk through the upcoming \nweekend. This will put a strain on heat sensitives groups. The \nheat headlines have been extended through Friday evening at the \nmoment, with increasing confidence in the need for an \nadditional extension beyond Friday.\n\nFurthermore, the extreme heat has began to dry out soils across\nthe area. CPC highlights portions of southeast KS and southern\nMO as an area vulnerable to rapid onset drought. This is similar\nto \"flash\" droughts we have seen in past Summer's, where heat\nwaves coincidences with minimal rain. Furthermore, CPC depicts \na high risk for hazardous temperatures in the 8 to 14 day \noutlook (August 18-24). This further increases confidence in\nprolonged heat through mid-August. The remaining uncertainty is \non the extent of heat as we get into early next week as ensemble\nguidance suggests another dip in the jet stream and slightly\ncooler temperatures. There is a scenario where we see a wide \nvariance in temperatures from north to south across the area. \nCheck back for more details.",
